course: dictionary
lesson: 386
title: Apartment: a home on one floor
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An apartment is a set of rooms for living in, usually on one floor of a bigger building. Context clues can show you this: «She lives in a top-floor apartment in a tall building.» The words top-floor and tall building tell you the apartment is one level of a bigger building.
explain: Apartments can also mean a set of rooms for a special use. «The king showed us the royal apartments in the palace.» Royal means belonging to a king or queen, and a palace is a very big home, so royal apartments are rooms used by the royal family.
explain: Some words mean the same thing in different countries. Britain says flat, and America says apartment. Other pairs work the same way: lift and elevator, torch and flashlight. Context helps you work out which one you are reading.
fact: An apartment is a home with rooms on one floor of a bigger building.
fact: «Apartment» can also mean a set of rooms for a special use, such as royal apartments in a palace.
fact: In Britain people often say flat, and in America they often say apartment.
fact: In maths, apartments can be counted by floors: 6 floors with 4 apartments each makes 24 apartments.
fact: In a fire, use the stairs, not the lift, and know your apartment number.
exercise: «She lives in a top-floor apartment in a tall building.» Where does she live? | In a tent in the garden | Under the ground | In a field | *On the top floor of a tall building
exercise: In Britain, which word means the same as apartment? | Torch | Lift | *Flat | Queue
exercise: «In a fire, you should use the stairs, not the lift.» Why? | Lifts are cold | The lift is faster in smoke | *The lift can stop working, and stairs are safer | Stairs are only for fun
test: «The king showed us the royal apartments in the palace.» What are royal apartments? | Rooms with no windows | Gardens with flowers | Stables for horses | *Rooms for a special use, in a palace
test: Ali's dad says lift, and his friend in New York says elevator. What do they mean? | *The same thing, a machine that carries you between floors | A ladder | Two different machines | A window
exam: «A girl in a New York apartment never sits on the windowsill, so she cannot climb up and fall.» Why does she keep her desk away from the window? | So the sun cannot shine on it | Because she likes the view less | *So she cannot climb up and fall | Because desks are heavy
